WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.640
Identity design should be part of MCP planning from the start.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.980
An integration may act as the user, as the application, or as a service account.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.680
Those choices affect auditability, least privilege, and user expectations.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.380
User identity answers who requested the work.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.280
Application identity answers which host or product is making the call.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.900
Service identity answers what backend component is performing the integration.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.200
Good design keeps these identities distinct enough to reason about authorization.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:35.900
Delegated access is a common pattern where an application acts on behalf of a user within defined limits.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.640
Service accounts may be appropriate for system-level workflows, but they should be narrowly scoped and monitored.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.900
Secrets and tokens should be protected from prompts, logs, and unnecessary tool exposure.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:56.080
Least privilege means each server, tool, or credential receives only the permissions needed for its intended use, in the intended context, for the intended users.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.060
Consent and user approval are different from model preference.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:06.920
A model may propose an action, but the application should verify whether the action is allowed and whether the user intended it.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.100
For low-risk read-only requests, policy may allow execution without interruption.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.200
For higher-impact actions, the user may need a clear approval prompt showing what will happen.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:22.140
Approval should be specific enough to matter, not a vague blanket acceptance of future activity.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.000
Tenant and workspace boundaries also matter.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:31.220
A user in one workspace should not gain access to another workspace because a tool or resource is broadly connected.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.680
A model-generated action request should not bypass application authorization.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:40.060
MCP integrations should not rely on the model as the final permission system.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:48.600
The stable rule is simple: the model can help decide what might be useful, but identity-aware application logic and downstream policy decide what is allowed.
